if you've been following along with this  series you've got a pretty good  understanding of the different  techniques that cloud eop uses to find  hide and protect sensitive data today  we'll find out how easy it is to build  and reuse your commonly used dlp  configurations  stay tuned  [Music]  [Applause]  kitty catwalks has already been using  dlp to inspect and de-identify  different data sources like archive chat  log files  issues from their ticketing system  databases and data that they collect  from customers for billing  chances are if you're anything like  kitty cat walks the data you want to  protect  may be from different sources and you  may want to inspect and de-identify that  data consistently using the same  configuration  each time this is where dlp templates  come in  templates allow you to save dop  configuration details  and reuse them against any data that you  might have essentially  decoupling configuration information  from the implementation of your request  there are two types of templates an  inspection template is going to save  any configuration having to do the  process of inspecting your data  like what info types you want to  discover a likelihood threshold  maximum number of findings to return and  exclusion rules  you can even use an inspection template  to define your own custom info type  let's see what it looks like to create a  template with some node.js code  here's an example of a request that will  send the create inspect template  function  to create an inspection template that's  configured to find phone numbers  and email addresses with a minimum  likelihood of  possible once it's been created  here's what that template looks like in  our cloud console  there's the info types we specified and  the likelihood too  let's edit our inspection template to  also include person name  we go here where it says manage info  types and enter  person name in the search box  select it and click done  now that we've saved our change our  inspection template details page  specifies three  info types note this template path at  the top of the page  we'll use that in a second to see our  template in action  let's check out this inspection request  instead of constructing an inspection  configuration object  for this inspection request we can  simply reference the inspection template  we created using the path name  like this our response of course will be  the results of the inspection  as expected we've got a phone number an  email and a name this is  really useful if you have several  inspection jobs set up against different  data sources that inspect for the same  info types  instead of editing each inspection  configuration separately you can simply  update the template details hold up a  second  what about the identification well the  second type of dlp template is a  de-identification template  a de-identification template is going to  save any configuration having to do the  process  of de-identifying your data for every  positive result  in a de-identification request the  template will have a corresponding  transformation to apply to it this is  also where you would specify  transformation specific customizations  like replacement strings or encryption  keys  the identification templates can be  applied not only to inspection results  but also to values in the specified  field in a database table  [Music]  here's an example of how we can use the  create de-identify template function to  create  a new de-identification template  here we're specifying that we want to  replace email addresses with the  corresponding info type  and we also want to make sure that the  names are replaced with custom  replacement value  once it's been created here's what the  template looks like back in our cloud  console  each info type we specified and its  transformation is listed here  as a transformation rule just like our  inspect template  we can edit it here to include  additional info types  let's edit this template to apply  masking to phone numbers  down here we'll add a transformation  rule  and choose mask with character now we  can configure our masking for this  transformation rule  we'll specify the info type phone number  down here where it says  manage info types now that it's saved  there's our new transformation rule  the identification templates include a  test page where we can get a quick  preview of the transformations we  configured in the template  emails will be replaced with info type  names are going to be replaced with a  replacement string  and phone numbers will be masked with  hash symbols  now let's see our de-identification  template do its thing in a  de-identification request  instead of including a de-identify  config block in our request  we can reference our de-identification  template once again using the path name  from the details page  you'll notice that we're referencing our  inspection template from before to  specify what info types we're inspecting  for  and here is a response from the request  the inspection template found a phone  number name and email address  and a data notification template applied  the transformations we specified  accordingly using templates is a  powerful way to decouple your inspection  and de-identification configuration from  your workflows  and your implementation inspection  templates can be configured to inspect  up to 150 built-in and custom info types  and the identification templates can use  any combination of the identification  techniques to de-identify  the results we use a really simple  example  but the value of templates really shines  for kitty cat walks when they got their  data that needs to be inspected and  de-identified  coming from their web and mobile apps  archived on gcs buckets  and stored on a number of databases  reviewing templates was a great way to  kind of bring everything together  next time we'll wrap things up with an  extended episode where we'll dive into  configuring dlp in the google cloud  console  see you soon  [Music]  you
 

